        "Gas pressure is the steady drumbeat of molecular collisions on container walls: each impact transfers momentum, and billions per second produce a smooth push. Raising temperature raises molecular speed, so collisions come harder and more often; raising density packs more colliders per cubic metre. Explaining pressure this way, rather than as a static push, is a defining habit of {S}.",
        "Boyle's law: at fixed temperature the pressure of a fixed mass of gas is inversely proportional to its volume, P1V1 = P2V2. Compressing 0.03 m^3 at 2.0 x 10^5 Pa into 0.01 m^3 raises the pressure to 6.0 x 10^5 Pa. The graph of pressure against volume is a hyperbola. A bicycle pump warms because compression work, not friction alone, raises the gas pressure, a distinction {S} examines.",
