        "An atom holds a tiny nucleus of protons and neutrons around which electrons move. Protons carry +1 charge, electrons -1, neutrons none; a proton or neutron has mass about 1 u while an electron is about 1/1836 u, so nearly all mass sits in the nucleus. In neutral atoms the electron number equals the proton number, the first balance every {S} calculation checks.",
        "Atomic number Z counts protons and fixes the element; mass number A counts nucleons. Isotopes share Z but differ in A: carbon-12 and carbon-13, chlorine-35 and chlorine-37. Chlorine's 75 to 25 abundance averages to relative atomic mass 35.5, which is why {S} tables quote non-integer atomic masses for many elements. The extra neutron changes mass and not chemistry, a distinction {S} examiners reward.",
