        "Iron-56 has Z = 26 and A = 56, so it holds 30 neutrons. The ion Fe3+ still has 26 protons but only 23 electrons, giving charge +3. Reading Z, A and the charge correctly converts a symbol into a full census of particles, the entry-level task of {S} nuclear questions.",
        "Calcium, Z = 20, fills 2, 8, 8, 2. Losing two electrons gives the Ca2+ ion with the argon arrangement, 2, 8, 8, which is stable. Calcium oxide then forms from Ca2+ and O2-, a 1:1 lattice. From configuration to ion to formula, this chain is how {S} builds compounds from the table.",
