        "Compute a photon's energy at 7.2 x 10^14 Hz: E = 6.63 x 10^-34 x 7.2 x 10^14 = 4.77 x 10^-19 J, or about 3.0 eV. If the metal's work function is 1.9 eV, the electrons leave with 3.0 - 1.9 = 1.1 eV. Fixing units before the subtraction is the discipline {S} demands in photoelectric sums.",
        "A source holds 80 g of a nuclide with half-life 5 days. After 15 days, three half-lives have passed, leaving 10 g. The count rate follows the same curve, so a detector reading 320 counts per second at the start reads 40 after 15 days. Successive halving, not straight lines, is the {S} decay pattern.",
        "Balance the alpha decay of radium-226: mass 226 - 4 = 222, atomic number 88 - 2 = 86, so radon-222 plus a helium-4 nucleus. For beta decay of carbon-14: mass stays 14, atomic number rises to 7, giving nitrogen-14. Conservation of nucleons and charge completes every {S} nuclear equation.",
